1. HR Metrics & KPI Analytics
Project: HR KPI Command Center
Students receive employee master data covering department, location, designation, joining date, salary, performance, attendance and exit status.
They calculate headcount, FTE, turnover/attrition rate, absenteeism, retention rate, average tenure, span of control, internal mobility, promotion rate, cost per employee and diversity representation.
Tools: Excel initially → Power BI later
Deliverable: Monthly HR KPI scorecard and management dashboard.

2. Excel for HR Analytics
Project: Employee Workforce Analysis in Excel
Give students a 1,000–2,000 employee dataset containing deliberate quality problems.
Students perform cleaning, duplicate detection, missing-value treatment, employee segmentation, XLOOKUP, IF/IFS, SUMIFS/COUNTIFS, date calculations, PivotTables, PivotCharts, conditional formatting and basic statistical analysis.
Business question: “What are the major workforce issues management should address?”
Deliverable: Cleaned dataset + interactive Excel HR dashboard.

3. Recruitment Analytics
Project: Recruitment Funnel & Hiring Effectiveness
Dataset can contain applications, source, recruiter, job role, interview stages, offer, joining status, salary, hiring cost and dates.
Students calculate:

Applications → screened → interviewed → offered → joined
Time-to-fill and time-to-hire
Offer acceptance rate
Source effectiveness
Cost per hire
Recruiter productivity
Candidate drop-off
Quality of hire

Tools: Excel / SQL / Power BI
Deliverable: Recruitment Funnel Dashboard with recommendations.

4. SQL for HR Analytics
Project: HR Employee Database Analytics
Create tables such as Employees, Departments, Attendance, Performance, Recruitment, Compensation, Training and Attrition.
Students answer business questions using SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY, CASE, JOINs, subqueries, CTEs and window functions.
Examples include finding departments with highest attrition, salary differences by role, top performers, employees without promotions, absenteeism trends and average tenure before exit.
Deliverable: HR SQL query portfolio containing 25–40 business questions.

5. Employee Attrition & Retention
Project: Why Are Employees Leaving?
This can become one of the flagship projects.
Students investigate attrition against compensation, tenure, overtime, manager, travel, satisfaction, promotion history, performance, commute distance and department.

Tools: Excel → Power BI → Python
Deliverable: Attrition diagnostic dashboard followed by a predictive model.
This is particularly useful because the same business problem progresses through descriptive, diagnostic and predictive analytics.

6. Power BI HR Analytics
Project: Executive Workforce Analytics Dashboard
Build a multi-page dashboard:
Executive Overview → Workforce → Recruitment → Attrition → Performance → Attendance → Compensation
Students learn data modelling, relationships, Power Query, visualization, drill-through, slicers, bookmarks and tooltips.
Deliverable: Management-ready .pbix dashboard.

7. DAX for HR Analytics
Project: Dynamic Workforce KPI Engine
Rather than teaching DAX separately, students extend the Power BI project.
Measures could include current headcount, YTD hires, YTD exits, attrition %, rolling 12-month attrition, average tenure, YoY headcount growth, absenteeism %, promotion rate, gender representation and hiring conversion.
A particularly useful challenge is “Headcount as of selected date”, because it teaches students why HR analytics is not simply counting rows.

8. Performance Analytics
Project: Employee Performance & High-Potential Analysis
Analyze ratings, goals achieved, manager ratings, competency scores, tenure, training and promotions.
Students identify performance distribution, high performers, consistently declining performers, department/manager differences and possible rating anomalies.
An advanced version can create a Performance × Potential 9-Box Grid.

Tools: Excel / Power BI / Python
Deliverable: Performance and talent-management dashboard.

9. Compensation Analytics

Project: Compensation & Pay Equity Dashboard
Analyze salary by role, grade, experience, tenure, location and performance.
Students calculate compa-ratio, salary distribution, salary bands, internal equity, pay compression and outliers.
Python can later introduce regression to investigate which observable factors explain compensation differences.
Important teaching point: statistical differences should be investigated rather than automatically interpreted as discrimination.

10. Attendance & Absenteeism Analytics
Project: Workforce Attendance Early-Warning Dashboard
Dataset includes attendance, leave, shift, overtime, manager and employee information.
Students calculate absenteeism %, leave patterns, overtime trends, department comparisons and recurring absence patterns.
Business scenario: “Operations reports increasing absenteeism. Where is the problem concentrated?”
Deliverable: Attendance dashboard + management summary.

11. Learning & Development Analytics
Project: Does Training Actually Improve Performance?
Combine training records with pre/post assessment scores and performance information.
Measure training participation, completion rate, training hours, assessment improvement, training cost, performance change and training effectiveness.
Advanced students can compare trained versus comparable non-trained employees while discussing why simple correlation does not prove causation.

12. Workforce Planning
Project: Next-Year Workforce Requirement Forecast
Business provides projected growth by function.
Students estimate required headcount using current workforce, productivity, attrition, retirement/exit assumptions, hiring lead times and expected demand.

Create scenarios such as: Baseline / 10% Growth / 20% Growth / Hiring Freeze / High Attrition
Tools: Excel + Power BI + Python
Deliverable: Workforce planning simulator.

13. Employee Engagement Analytics
Project: Employee Engagement Survey Analyzer
Use Likert-scale survey responses and employee demographics.
Analyze engagement score, eNPS, satisfaction dimensions, department/manager differences and key engagement drivers.
Python can add correlation analysis and regression; text responses can later introduce NLP.

14. Python for HR Analytics
Project: HR Analytics Using Pandas
Students import the employee dataset and perform data cleaning, exploratory analysis, grouping, aggregation, missing-value treatment, outlier analysis, correlations and visualizations.

Libraries: Pandas → NumPy → Matplotlib → Scikit-learn
Rather than generic Python exercises, every notebook should answer an HR question.

15. Predictive Attrition Analytics
Project: Employee Attrition Prediction System
This should be the main ML project.
Problem: Predict whether an employee is at risk of leaving.

Students perform:
Raw Data → Cleaning → EDA → Feature Engineering → Train/Test Split → Model Training → Evaluation → Prediction → Business Interpretation
Models can include Logistic Regression, Decision Tree and Random Forest.
Evaluation should cover confusion matrix, accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and ROC-AUC.
Deliverable: Notebook + attrition risk list + intervention recommendations.

16. Recruitment Predictive Analytics
Project: Candidate Joining Probability
Predict whether a candidate who receives an offer will actually join.
Possible predictors include source, experience, offered salary, expected salary, notice period, location, interview score and recruitment duration.

ML concept: Classification.
This gives students a second classification use case beyond attrition.

17. Employee Performance Prediction
Project: Predict Future Employee Performance

Use tenure, previous ratings, training hours, attendance, experience, role and other appropriate job-related variables.
ML concept: Regression when predicting a numeric performance measure.
Students learn MAE, MSE, RMSE and R².

18. Employee Segmentation
Project: Discover Workforce Personas
Use unsupervised learning to group employees based on suitable job-related variables such as tenure, compensation, training, performance and engagement.
ML concept: K-Means clustering.

Students could discover descriptive clusters such as newer employees with high training participation or experienced high performers.
The important lesson is to interpret clusters rather than blindly label people.

19. AI / Generative AI in HR
Project: HR Policy Assistant using RAG
Provide HR policy documents covering leave, travel, WFH, reimbursement, probation and benefits.
Students build a simple assistant where an employee can ask:
“How many days of parental leave am I eligible for?”
The system retrieves the relevant policy content before answering.
Concepts: LLM + embeddings + vector search + RAG + grounding.
This is much stronger than merely demonstrating ChatGPT prompts.

20. NLP for Employee Feedback
Project: Employee Voice Analyzer
Analyze hundreds or thousands of anonymized employee survey comments.
Students perform sentiment analysis, topic/theme extraction, keyword analysis and management summarization.
Tools: Python + NLP/LLM
Deliverable: Employee Voice Dashboard showing major themes while protecting individual privacy.

21. AI-Assisted Resume Analysis
Project: Job–Resume Skills Matching Lab
Students extract job-related skills from a job description and compare them with candidate resumes.
The educational emphasis should be on skills extraction, explainability, human review and bias testing, rather than building an autonomous hiring decision-maker.
Deliverable: Candidate–job skills-gap report.

22. HR Data Quality
Project: HR Data Quality Audit
Deliberately corrupt an HR dataset with missing department codes, duplicate employee IDs, impossible joining dates, inconsistent job titles, invalid salaries and unmatched manager IDs.
Students build rules to detect and report problems.
Tools: Excel → SQL → Python.
This is an excellent early project because real HR analytics starts with messy data.

23. HR Reporting Automation
Project: Automated Monthly HR Report
Students receive monthly HR data and automate:
Data → Cleaning → KPI Calculation → Charts → Management Report
Python can generate summary tables automatically while Power BI handles refreshable reporting.
This teaches the difference between analysis and operational analytics.

24. Prescriptive HR Analytics
Project: Retention Intervention Simulator
Go beyond predicting attrition.
Suppose the company has ₹25 lakh available for retention interventions.
Students determine which employee groups warrant further review and simulate interventions such as manager action, career-development conversations, learning opportunities or compensation review.

The challenge becomes: Prediction → Business decision → Resource constraint → Expected impact

25. Final Capstone
Project: People Analytics 360 – From HR Data to Management Decision
Interconnected datasets: Employee Master + Recruitment + Attendance + Compensation + Performance + Training + Engagement + Exit

Students then build:
Excel: Data quality audit and exploratory analysis
SQL: HR analytical database and business queries
Power BI: Executive People Analytics dashboard
DAX: Dynamic HR KPI measures
Python: Attrition prediction / workforce forecasting
AI: Employee feedback or HR policy analysis
Business: 5–10 recommendations for HR leadership

The final presentation can simulate a CHRO meeting: “You have 15 minutes with the CHRO. What does the data say, why does it matter, and what should management investigate or do next?”
